    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "This is Liam, in for Bear. Imagine a perfect quantum photocopier: feed it any unknown state, add a blank qubit, and get two exact copies. One line of algebra jams the machine forever.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "CARD",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "type",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "Why can't a unitary machine perfectly copy every unknown qubit?",
            "topic": "QUANTUM MECHANICS — NO CLONING",
            "segment": "The Quantum Photocopier Jams",
            "greeting": "Shalom, Liam",
            "runningText": "testing the overlap…",
            "output": [
              "Unitarity preserves input overlap.",
              "Perfect copying would square it."
            ],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Claude",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 11.16,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "Call the unknown input psi and the blank state zero. The proposed unitary copier must turn psi-zero into psi-psi — without learning what psi is.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "transform"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 9.22,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "The same fixed machine must also copy another possible input phi: phi-zero becomes phi-phi. Universal means the hardware cannot be redesigned after someone reveals the state.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "compare"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 10.77,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "Now compare the two possible inputs. Their inner product, z, measures quantum overlap. Zero means perfectly distinguishable; a magnitude between zero and one means nonorthogonal.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "scan"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 10.99,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "Unitary evolution preserves inner products. So the overlap between the two input branches must equal the overlap between the two output branches.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "hold"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 8.53,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "But the output contains two copies. Tensor-product overlaps multiply, so the output overlap is z times z — z squared.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "accumulate"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 7.94,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B06.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "estimated_duration_s": 9,
      "narration_text": "The copier therefore demands z equals z squared. Move everything to one side: z times one minus z equals zero.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "transform"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B07.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 7.53,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B07.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B08",
      "estimated_duration_s": 8,
      "narration_text": "Only z equals zero or z equals one survives. The whole continuum of partly overlapping states breaks the machine.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "collapse"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B08.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 7.57,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B08.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B09",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "Orthogonal states can be copied by a device built for that basis — a CNOT copies computational-basis zero and one. Identical inputs are trivial. What fails is one deterministic perfect device for arbitrary unknown nonorthogonal states.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "compare"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B09.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 15.19,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B09.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B10",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "That qualifier matters. If you know the state, you can prepare another. Approximate cloners and probabilistic schemes also exist. The theorem bans universal, exact, deterministic cloning.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "branch"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B10.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 11.24,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B10.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B11",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "And measuring first does not rescue the copier. Nonorthogonal states cannot be perfectly identified from one specimen, and measurement generally changes the state you hoped to preserve.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "collapse"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B11.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 10.62,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B11.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B12",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "The verdict: unitarity keeps similarity fixed, while copying would multiply similarity by itself. Those two rules agree only at the endpoints — identical or perfectly distinguishable states.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "accumulate"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B12.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 11.56,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B12.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B13",
      "estimated_duration_s": 17,
      "narration_text": "Your turn. Paste this into Claude: Prove the no-cloning theorem using two nonorthogonal qubit states with overlap one half, then show why CNOT can still copy zero and one. Ask it to label exactly where universality enters the proof.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "type",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "Prove no-cloning using two states with overlap 1/2, then show why CNOT can copy |0> and |1>. Label where universality enters.",
            "topic": "YOUR TURN",
            "segment": "Break the Photocopier",
            "greeting": "Your turn.",
            "runningText": "paste this into Claude…",
            "output": [
              "Compare z with z^2 at z=1/2.",
              "Then test the orthogonal endpoint."
            ],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Claude",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B13.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B13.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 13.91,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B13.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:30:53"
      }
    },
